GSM and XCOPA across 6-8 typologically diverse languages and 3-4 open-weight models (Qwen2.5, Llama-3.1, Aya), segment CoT into steps and plot LAS trajectories; test correlation between step-level difficulty proxies (token surprisal, operation complexity for math) and LAS. (3) Causal difficulty test: Use truncation (feed partial CoT) and controlled error-injection into a single step; measure whether LAS spikes at genuinely hard/error-recovery steps vs. easy steps. (4) Correctness prediction: Train a simple probe on intermediate-step LAS features to predict final correctness; report AUROC vs. baselines (confidence, entropy, logit-lens English-routing degree). (5) DG-LAS intervention: Flag high-difficulty steps online (via surprisal threshold or the probe), and at those steps only, apply activation steering that increases LAS (subtract language-identity component in middle layers). Compare four conditions: no intervention, blanket ablation (prior SOTA), random-gated steering (control), and DG-LAS. Metrics: exact-match accuracy per language, output fluency (perplexity under an external LM, human/GPT judge on a sample), and language fidelity (answer-in-target-language rate). (6) Ablations: layer choice, gating threshold, steering strength; and cross-language generalization of the identity subspace.', 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'The language-identity subspace may entangle content with language, confounding LAS; we mitigate with parallel-data controls and orthogonalization. Step segmentation is noisy for languages with weak CoT structure. Difficulty proxies (surprisal) may not reflect true reasoning difficulty. Difficulty-gated steering could destabilize generation or reduce fluency if applied too aggressively; strength/threshold tuning is required. Gains may be model- or language-specific and small on high-resource languages. Causal claims from steering are correlational unless carefully controlled, so we include random-gated and blanket baselines to isolate the difficulty-gating contribution.'}

