ctivation patching / attention-head + MLP-neuron ablation to identify components whose removal selectively drops hierarchical grammaticality accuracy while sparing linear accuracy. Record the ranked set S_syn of hierarchy-selective components per model. (2) Control circuits: identify (a) linear/positional-selective components S_lin, (b) semantic/lexical-selective components S_sem (localized via a synonym/antonym or category-membership contrast), and (c) random-matched-size sets S_rand. (3) Non-linguistic reasoning suite with controllable depth: order-of-operations arithmetic (nesting depth 0-5), nested boolean logic (depth 0-5), balanced-parenthesis validity (depth 1-8), and Python-expression evaluation (AST depth 1-6). Generate ~2000 items per task, balanced by depth. (4) Cross-domain ablation test: measure accuracy vs. depth under ablation of S_syn, S_lin, S_sem, S_rand, and no-ablation. Key metric: the depth x ablation interaction coefficient from a mixed-effects logistic regression (accuracy ~ depth * ablation_set + model). Prediction: S_syn produces a significantly steeper accuracy drop with depth than all controls. (5) Component-overlap analysis: for each non-linguistic task, independently localize causally-important components and compute Jaccard overlap with S_syn vs. S_lin/S_sem/S_rand, testing whether reasoning recruits the syntax circuit above chance. (6) Converse transfer: fine-tune (LoRA) on deep nested-reasoning items, then re-measure hierarchical grammaticality accuracy on held-out center-embedding sentences and re-measure S_syn ablation sensitivity; test whether the linguistic hierarchy circuit strengthens. (7) Models: Qwen2.5 (1.5B/7B), Llama-3.1-8B, Pythia (for a training-stage variant if time permits). Metrics: exact-match accuracy, depth x ablation interaction, Jaccard overlap, and pre/post transfer effect sizes with bootstrap CIs.', 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "(1) Ablating syntax components may broadly degrade all outputs, masking the selective depth effect; we mitigate with matched-size random and semantic control ablations and by focusing on the *interaction* with depth rather than absolute drops. (2) Hierarchy-selective localization may be noisy or model-specific; we report multiple models and cross-validate localization on held-out sentence sets. (3) Non-linguistic tasks still pass through tokenization/language machinery, so 'non-linguistic' is relative; we use nonce-word controls and symbolic tokens to reduce semantic confounds. (4) Depth and difficulty may be confounded with sequence length; we length-match items across depths. (5) A null result (no cross-domain reuse) is still publishable as evidence that the structure engine is domain-specific, but weakens the headline claim. (6) LoRA transfer effects may be small; we power the experiment with sufficient items and report effect sizes with CIs."}

