Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "token_granularity_reasoning_tradeoff", "Title": "The Granularity Tax: When Coarse Tokenization Buys Fluency at the Cost of Compositional Reasoning", "Short Hypothesis": "The token granularity at which a concept is encoded controls a fundamental trade-off in LLMs: coarse (multi-character, whole-word) tokens improve fluency and retrieval but suppress the compositional/algorithmic reasoning that requires manipulating a concept's internal parts. Crucially, this trade-off is not a fixed property of the tokenizer but is dynamically re-negotiated inside the model, and we can measure a per-concept 'granularity depth' at which sub-token structure becomes accessible and causally intervene on it to trade fluency for reasoning at inference time, without retraining.", "Related Work": "A cluster of recent work establishes that tokenization harms character-level and arithmetic reasoning: character understanding emerges late and depends on vocabulary size (The Strawberry Problem, 2025; How Do LMs Acquire Character-Level Information, 2026), spelling knowledge is reconstructed in intermediate layers with a 'breakthrough layer' (Spelling-out is not Straightforward, 2025), and tokenization bounds symbolic/arithmetic reasoning even with CoT (Tokenization Constraints, 2025; Tokenization counts, 2024; Counting Ability and Tokenization, 2024). Separately, scaling-law work argues LARGER vocabularies (coarser tokens) improve overall performance (Scaling Laws with Vocabulary, 2024; Over-Tokenized Transformer, 2025). These two literatures are in tension but never reconciled: the first says coarse tokens hurt reasoning, the second says coarse tokens help. No prior work (a) frames these as a single controllable trade-off along a granularity axis, (b) measures WHERE in depth sub-token structure is re-exposed as a per-concept quantity ('granularity depth'), or (c) offers a training-free inference-time intervention that shifts a model along the fluency-vs-reasoning trade-off by amplifying sub-token reconstruction. We reconcile the tension and turn it into a controllable lever.", "Abstract": "Two recent threads of tokenization research point in opposite directions: one shows that coarse subword tokens cripple character-level and symbolic reasoning, while scaling-law work shows that larger vocabularies (coarser tokens) improve overall model performance. We argue these are two faces of a single phenomenon we call the granularity tax: encoding a concept in a coarse token improves fluent retrieval but hides the concept's internal parts, suppressing the compositional reasoning that must manipulate those parts. We make three contributions. First, we show empirically that for the SAME underlying string, forcing coarse vs. fine tokenization produces a systematic fluency-up / compositional-reasoning-down trade-off across arithmetic, spelling, anagram, and morphological tasks. Second, we introduce granularity depth: using probing classifiers we measure, per concept, the layer at which a coarse token's sub-token structure becomes linearly decodable, and show it is later for higher-frequency/coarser tokens, quantifying an internal 're-negotiation' of granularity. Third, we introduce a training-free intervention, Sub-Token Reconstruction Steering (STRS): we identify the direction along which sub-token structure is written into the residual stream at the breakthrough layer and amplify it during inference. We show STRS shifts a model along the trade-off curve, recovering compositional-reasoning accuracy on coarse-token inputs at a measurable, tunable cost to fluency, and compare it against CoT, single-digit tokenization, and re-tokenization baselines across several open-weight models. Our work unifies conflicting tokenization findings into one controllable axis and provides both a diagnostic (granularity depth) and a lever (STRS) linking token granularity to machine reasoning.", "Experiments": "(1) Trade-off characterization: Construct a matched task battery where each item can be presented at coarse vs. fine granularity for the identical underlying content: (a) arithmetic (multi-digit tokens vs. comma/space-separated single digits), (b) spelling/letter-counting (whole-word token vs. spelled-out), (c) anagram/character-reordering, (d) morphological segmentation (whole word vs. morpheme-spaced). For each task, on 3-4 open-weight models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B, Pythia for a scale sweep), measure compositional-reasoning accuracy AND a fluency proxy (perplexity/log-prob of natural continuations, plus a downstream generation-quality check on held-out prose). Fit a per-model trade-off curve of accuracy vs. token coarseness. (2) Granularity-depth probing: For a set of tokens spanning frequency/length, collect residual-stream activations across layers on a spelling/decomposition prompt; train per-layer linear probes to decode the i-th character / sub-token constituent. Define granularity depth = earliest layer where decoding accuracy exceeds a threshold. Test correlation between granularity depth and (token frequency, token length, vocabulary rank), predicting later depth for coarser/high-frequency tokens. Cross-check the 'breakthrough layer' from prior work. (3) Causal link between granularity depth and reasoning: Test whether items whose relevant token has a later granularity depth are disproportionately failed on compositional tasks (mixed-effects model: accuracy ~ granularity_depth + token_length + frequency + model). (4) Sub-Token Reconstruction Steering (STRS): At the breakthrough layer, compute the difference-in-means direction between activations where sub-token structure is decodable vs. not (contrast pairs), giving a reconstruction direction; amplify it at inference on coarse-token inputs. Measure the resulting movement along the trade-off curve: compositional accuracy gain vs. fluency cost, swept over steering strength. (5) Baselines/controls: compare STRS to (i) no intervention, (ii) CoT prompting, (iii) forced fine tokenization (single-digit/spelled-out), (iv) random-direction steering, (v) re-tokenization. Metrics: task accuracy, fluency (perplexity + generation-quality judge on a sample), answer fidelity, and area-under trade-off-curve improvement (accuracy recovered per unit fluency lost). (6) Scale sweep on Pythia checkpoints: track when the granularity tax and the recoverability via STRS emerge during training and across model sizes.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "(1) Coarse vs. fine presentations may differ in more than granularity (e.g., sequence length, digit ordering); we length-control and use right-to-left/comma conventions from prior arithmetic work to isolate granularity. (2) The 'fluency' proxy (perplexity) may not fully capture generation quality; we supplement with a judged generation-quality check. (3) The reconstruction direction may be entangled with content; we use matched contrast pairs and random-direction controls. (4) STRS may broadly degrade the model if amplified too strongly; the point is a tunable trade-off, so we report the full strength sweep rather than a single operating point. (5) Granularity depth probing can be noisy for rare tokens; we report CIs and restrict to well-attested tokens. (6) A null STRS result (structure cannot be cheaply amplified) is still informative, confirming the tax is intrinsic rather than a readout artifact, and the trade-off characterization and granularity-depth diagnostic stand independently."}}
