prompting, (iii) forced fine tokenization (single-digit/spelled-out), (iv) random-direction steering, (v) re-tokenization. Metrics: task accuracy, fluency (perplexity + generation-quality judge on a sample), answer fidelity, and area-under trade-off-curve improvement (accuracy recovered per unit fluency lost). (6) Scale sweep on Pythia checkpoints: track when the granularity tax and the recoverability via STRS emerge during training and across model sizes.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "(1) Coarse vs. fine presentations may differ in more than granularity (e.g., sequence length, digit ordering); we length-control and use right-to-left/comma conventions from prior arithmetic work to isolate granularity. (2) The 'fluency' proxy (perplexity) may not fully capture generation quality; we supplement with a judged generation-quality check. (3) The reconstruction direction may be entangled with content; we use matched contrast pairs and random-direction controls. (4) STRS may broadly degrade the model if amplified too strongly; the point is a tunable trade-off, so we report the full strength sweep rather than a single operating point. (5) Granularity depth probing can be noisy for rare tokens; we report CIs and restrict to well-attested tokens. (6) A null STRS result (structure cannot be cheaply amplified) is still informative, confirming the tax is intrinsic rather than a readout artifact, and the trade-off characterization and granularity-depth diagnostic stand independently."}

