Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "lexical_gap_resolution",
  "Title": "Mind the Gap: Do Lexicalization Gaps in the Latent Pivot Language Blur an LLM's Concepts?",
  "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs route non-English inputs through a largely English-shaped 'concept space.' We hypothesize that a concept's internal representational resolution is bottlenecked by whether it is *lexicalized* (has a compact, single-/few-token word) in the model's latent pivot language: concepts that fall in a lexical gap of the pivot language are represented more diffusely, are harder to separate from neighbors, and yield systematically worse fine-grained reasoning \u2014 and providing an ad-hoc lexicalization (a coined single token/name) in-context sharpens both the geometry and the behavior.",
  "Related Work": "A cluster of work establishes that multilingual LLMs form a shared, largely English-pivoted concept space in middle layers (Semantic Hub Hypothesis 2024; Do Llamas Work in English 2024; Separating Tongue from Thought 2024; Tracing Multilingual Representations with Cross-Layer Transcoders 2025; When Meanings Meet 2026). These works show a shared space *exists* and can be causally patched, and that averaging concept representations across languages helps translation. Concept Space Alignment (2024) and High-Dimensional Interlingual Representations (2025) show alignment quality varies by typology and abstractness. XCOMPS (2025) shows conceptual understanding degrades for low-resource and morphologically complex languages. Crucially, all this work treats concepts holistically or averages across languages; none isolates *lexicalization gaps* \u2014 concepts that are compactly lexicalized in some languages but not the pivot \u2014 as a natural, controllable experiment on whether the pivot language's vocabulary sets a resolution limit on non-linguistic conceptual discrimination and reasoning. We uniquely (1) predict a lexicalization-gap penalty on representational resolution and downstream fine-grained reasoning, (2) contrast this with mere frequency/abstractness confounds, and (3) test a causal, training-free 'ad-hoc lexicalization' intervention that closes the gap.",
  "Abstract": "Multilingual language models process the world through a shared internal concept space that is disproportionately shaped by their dominant (often English) latent pivot language. We ask a simple question with deep implications for how language bounds machine intelligence: does the *vocabulary* of the pivot language set a resolution limit on what the model can finely discriminate and reason about? Human languages differ in which concepts they lexicalize \u2014 some have a single compact word for a notion (e.g., a specific kinship relation, an emotion, a color category, a shade of taste) that other languages must express periphrastically. We hypothesize a lexicalization-gap penalty: concepts that lack a compact lexicalization in the pivot language are represented more diffusely (lower cluster tightness, poorer separability from neighbors) and are correspondingly harder for the model to reason about at fine grain, even when the content is expressed identically. We assemble concept sets that are lexicalized in some languages but constitute lexical gaps in English, matched to lexicalized controls on frequency and abstractness, and measure (i) representational resolution via probing and cluster geometry across layers, and (ii) fine-grained behavioral discrimination and relational reasoning. We then test causality two ways: introducing an ad-hoc single-token lexicalization (a coined name) in-context should sharpen geometry and improve reasoning about gap concepts, while artificially 'de-lexicalizing' control concepts (forcing periphrastic reference) should blur them. Across several open-weight models we quantify the gap penalty, its dissociation from frequency/abstractness, and the effectiveness of ad-hoc lexicalization. Our work reframes the shared concept space from a uniform interlingua into a vocabulary-limited one, revealing a concrete channel by which language shapes AI intelligence.",
  "Experiments": "(1) Concept-set construction: Build ~120 target concepts that are compactly lexicalized in at least one non-English language but are English lexical gaps (require periphrasis), spanning domains: emotions (e.g., Portuguese 'saudade', German 'Schadenfreude'), kinship (languages with fine maternal/paternal/sibling-age distinctions), color/material terms, taste/texture terms, and social-relation terms. For each, build a matched control concept that IS compactly lexicalized in English, matched on estimated corpus frequency (via a reference corpus) and on abstractness rating. Each concept comes with (a) a natural-language definition/description usable across languages, and (b) a set of minimally different neighbor concepts for discrimination items. (2) Representational-resolution measurement: For each concept, present multiple paraphrased descriptions (in English and 2-3 other languages, back-translation checked) and extract middle-layer hidden states at matched positions on 3-4 open-weight models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B, Aya-23-8B). Compute per-concept cluster tightness (within-concept variance of representations across paraphrases), neighbor separability (silhouette score vs. minimally different neighbors), and probe decodability (linear probe AUROC distinguishing the concept from its neighbors). Key test: mixed-effects model, resolution ~ lexical_gap_status + frequency + abstractness + language + (1|model); prediction = significant negative lexical_gap coefficient beyond frequency/abstractness. (3) Behavioral fine-grained reasoning: For each concept build discrimination and relational items \u2014 odd-one-out among near-neighbors, 'does description X match concept better than concept-neighbor?', and analogy/relational-ordering tasks \u2014 all expressed via descriptions so the surface never contains a compact English word. Measure accuracy; test lexical_gap penalty controlling for frequency/abstractness; test whether per-concept behavioral penalty correlates with per-concept representational-resolution penalty (Spearman). (4) Causal ad-hoc lexicalization: Introduce in-context a coined single-token name for a gap concept ('Let X = <definition>') and re-present the same discrimination/relational items using X; measure change in cluster tightness, separability, and accuracy vs. a no-name baseline and a matched-length inert-preamble control. Prediction: naming sharpens geometry and improves accuracy for gap concepts. (5) Converse de-lexicalization: For control (English-lexicalized) concepts, force periphrastic reference (never use the compact word, only its definition) and test whether resolution/accuracy degrade toward gap-concept levels. (6) Pivot-language check: For a model with a non-English latent pivot (e.g., an available Japanese-continued-pretraining model), test whether the gap penalty tracks lexicalization in *that* pivot language rather than English, directly linking the effect to the pivot language's vocabulary. Metrics: cluster tightness, silhouette/separability, probe AUROC, discrimination/relational accuracy, gap-penalty coefficients with bootstrap CIs, resolution-to-behavior correlation, and naming/de-lexicalization effect sizes.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "(1) Frequency and abstractness are hard to fully control and may drive apparent gap penalties; we match controls on both and include them as covariates, and use the causal naming/de-lexicalization interventions (which hold the concept fixed) to isolate lexicalization per se. (2) 'Lexical gap' status is fuzzy and language-dependent; we rely on multiple annotators and cross-linguistic dictionaries and report robustness to concept-set choices. (3) Extracting clean per-concept representations is noisy given surface-token contamination; we use many paraphrases, matched positions, and multiple layers. (4) Ad-hoc naming might help simply by adding a stable anchor token regardless of concepts, so we include inert-preamble and random-name controls. (5) The pivot-language crossover test depends on availability of a non-English-pivot model; if unavailable we rely on the English-pivot evidence plus the causal interventions. (6) A null result (no lexicalization-gap penalty) is itself informative, indicating the shared concept space is genuinely vocabulary-independent and that periphrastic descriptions fully recover fine-grained conceptual resolution."
}}
