lling for frequency/abstractness; test whether per-concept behavioral penalty correlates with per-concept representational-resolution penalty (Spearman). (4) Causal ad-hoc lexicalization: Introduce in-context a coined single-token name for a gap concept ('Let X = <definition>') and re-present the same discrimination/relational items using X; measure change in cluster tightness, separability, and accuracy vs. a no-name baseline and a matched-length inert-preamble control. Prediction: naming sharpens geometry and improves accuracy for gap concepts. (5) Converse de-lexicalization: For control (English-lexicalized) concepts, force periphrastic reference (never use the compact word, only its definition) and test whether resolution/accuracy degrade toward gap-concept levels. (6) Pivot-language check: For a model with a non-English latent pivot (e.g., an available Japanese-continued-pretraining model), test whether the gap penalty tracks lexicalization in *that* pivot language rather than English, directly linking the effect to the pivot language's vocabulary. Metrics: cluster tightness, silhouette/separability, probe AUROC, discrimination/relational accuracy, gap-penalty coefficients with bootstrap CIs, resolution-to-behavior correlation, and naming/de-lexicalization effect sizes.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "(1) Frequency and abstractness are hard to fully control and may drive apparent gap penalties; we match controls on both and include them as covariates, and use the causal naming/de-lexicalization interventions (which hold the concept fixed) to isolate lexicalization per se. (2) 'Lexical gap' status is fuzzy and language-dependent; we rely on multiple annotators and cross-linguistic dictionaries and report robustness to concept-set choices. (3) Extracting clean per-concept representations is noisy given surface-token contamination; we use many paraphrases, matched positions, and multiple layers. (4) Ad-hoc naming might help simply by adding a stable anchor toke